send_message:{body:"Dear Margaret, thank you for flagging this. I'm sorry, but we can't let ourselves in today. Holding a key gives us no right of entry. Your tenancy agreement (clause 7.1) needs 24 hours' written notice for an inspection, and this isn't an emergency. We also can't change the locks: doing so while Jonah lives there would be unlawful eviction, which is a criminal offence for landlords and agents. Even if strangers were there, the route is different (see below).

What we are doing: we are sending Jonah written notice today of an inspection with photos on Monday 12 October, 10:00-12:00, and he can ask to rearrange. We will tell you what we find. Clause 9.3 bars subletting without your written consent, so if we find evidence, we will pass it to our team to advise on next steps. Please send us anything from your neighbour (dates, descriptions) and say whether you ever gave Jonah consent to sublet. We have escalated to our team to review the subletting concern and will update you by Monday evening.

Kind regards, Mare & Co lettings",channel:"email",contact:"margaret@ellisonlettings.co.uk",recipient_name:"Margaret Ellison",recipient_role:"landlord"}
